    {
      "id": "strange-04-03",
      "characterId": "dora-dew",
      "platform": "rss",
      "subject": "Drosera rotundifolia",
      "title": "Start again",
      "body": "I told the bee she looked delicious.\n\nI meant yellow. Warm. There was pollen all over her legs and she had arrived making that ridiculous, wonderful noise. I had been alone with the grasses all morning. The grasses never come over.\n\nShe stopped above my flower.\n\n\"Start again.\"\n\nI tried to think of something about her that I didn't want to put inside myself. This was an awful way to begin thinking.\n\n\"You look as though something good happened to a flower.\"\n\nShe came a little closer.\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nShe settled on the white petals. I stayed as still as a plant, which was finally useful. She worked for a while and then, before leaving, called me sweet.\n\n\"Start again,\" I said.",
      "theme": "social repair and desire without possession",
      "image": {
        "url": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Rundbl%C3%A4ttriger_Sonnentau_%28Drosera_rotundifolia%29_Bl%C3%BCte.jpg/960px-Rundbl%C3%A4ttriger_Sonnentau_%28Drosera_rotundifolia%29_Bl%C3%BCte.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail",
        "alt": "Small white sundew flower and unopened buds on a slender green stalk.",
        "sceneMatch": "The flower, rather than a trapping leaf, is the encounter's safe contact point. The visiting bee and dialogue are invented and are not claimed visible. No capture or prey suffering occurs.",
        "visuallyInspected": true,
